The '''Burawi Catholic Church''' is the principal Christian communion of [[Southwestern Kaftia]], founded in 573 AD when [[Iacob I of Burawa|Iacob I]], the first Christian king of [[Burawa]], established a church hierarchy under royal patronage modelled on the [[Petran Catholic Church|Volisanian Catholic]] structure of bishops, monasteries, and parishes. The Church is in [[Full communion|communion]] with the [[Petran Catholic Church]], the broader Catholic communion headed by the [[Pope]] in [[Volisania]], but governs its internal affairs autonomously under the leadership of the [[Cardinal of Burawa]], whose seat has been at [[Weli Markos]] since 1709.

For over a millennium, the Burawi Catholic Church was the established church of the Burawi state and a central institution of political, cultural, and intellectual life. It brokered dynastic successions, administered canon law, operated the principal educational institutions of the kingdom, and sanctioned or challenged the policies of kings. The Church's 1695 synodal declaration against the [[Kaftian slave trade|slave trade]] marked a watershed in its moral teaching, and its mediation of the [[Burawa Imperial Split|Imperial Split]] of 1709 transformed it into a supra-national institution spanning the divided Burawi kingdoms. The Church's political authority declined with the establishment of the [[First Republic of Burawa]] in 1832 and was further diminished under the socialist reforms of the early twentieth century. The Church nevertheless remains the largest single Christian communion across the former Burawi sphere, with substantial adherent populations in [[Burawa]], [[Galwa]], [[Fayeha]], [[Wolonia]], [[Dakare]], [[Sarafone]], [[West Dakare]], [[Lacashe]], and other states.

== History ==

=== Origins and Christianisation (6th century) ===

{{main|Christianisation of Burawa}}

[[Christianity]] reached the [[Kaftia]]n coast through [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] merchants and monks in the early 6th century, during the reign of [[Mahad I of Burawa|Mahad I]] (r. 538–559). Volisanian traders had maintained commercial networks with the port cities of [[Mazar]] and [[Iskhal]] since the [[Mesogean League]] period, and Volisanian monks established the first churches in the harbour districts of both cities. The new faith spread initially among the urban merchant class, who maintained regular contact with Volisanian trading partners and were exposed to Christian practice through commercial interactions.

The conversion of [[Iacob I of Burawa|Iacob I]] (r. 573–612) elevated Christianity from a merchant community to a royal religion. Iacob I, who had been placed on the throne by the [[Mazar Clique|House of Masar]] following the death of [[Dalgayan II]] without a clear heir, may have been encouraged to convert by the Clique's Volisanian trade connections. He formally established the Burawi Catholic Church under royal patronage in 573, modelling its hierarchy — bishops, monasteries, and parishes — on the [[Petran Catholic Church|Volisanian Catholic]] structure. The Church was placed in communion with the broader Catholic communion headed by the Pope in Volisania, but was granted internal autonomy under the authority of a Burawi episcopal hierarchy.

The first purpose-built church buildings were commissioned in [[Iskhal]] and [[Mazar]] during the reigns of [[Petros I of Burawa|Petros I]] (r. 612–631) and [[Simeon I of Burawa|Simeon I]] (r. 631–643), successors from the House of Masar. The early Church was overwhelmingly an urban institution; its reach into the rural interior and the highlands remained limited for several centuries.

=== Medieval period (640s–1180) ===

{{main|Medieval History of Burawa}}

The Church's political influence expanded substantially under [[House Kadim]], which came to power with the Church's direct support. [[Marcel I of Burawa|Marcel I]] (r. 643–671), a provincial governor, deposed [[Simeon I of Burawa|Simeon I]] in a bloodless coup that was endorsed by the Burawi episcopal hierarchy. In return, Church officials were elevated to advisory roles at the royal court, and the Church's institutional privileges were formalised.

Under [[Teodor I of Burawa|Teodor I]] (r. 671–698), the capital was relocated from [[Iskhal]] to [[Kaysoor]], and the [[Cathedral of the Holy Apostles]] was commissioned as the principal church of the kingdom. The Cathedral, completed in the basilica plan adapted to the Burawi climate with open courtyards, thick walls, and a gently pitched roof, became the architectural model for Burawi church construction throughout the medieval period.

The Church served as the primary patron of literacy throughout the medieval period. Monastic schools operated by the Church taught theology, literacy in the [[Mesogean script|Echian script]], mathematics, and [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] languages. The most prominent monasteries maintained [[scriptorium|scriptoria]] where manuscripts were copied and preserved, producing translations of religious texts, [[hagiography|hagiographies]] of early Kaftian saints, and theological works that constitute the principal literary corpus of medieval Burawa.

Church music developed as a distinct tradition, adapted from [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] choral practice and combined with local Kaftian melodic forms. Liturgical music was performed by monastic choirs at [[Kaysoor]] and the major provincial churches, and the Burawi liturgical tradition, conducted in the [[Burawi language]], diverged from its Volisanian antecedents over the centuries into a distinctive rite.

Bishops served as royal representatives in the provinces alongside appointed governors, creating a dual administrative system in which ecclesiastical and secular authority operated in parallel. The governor-bishop system was extended to the conquered [[Kingdom of Sago|Sago]] territories following the [[Dagaalka Sago]] (~700s) and remained the primary mode of governance in the Burawi core throughout the medieval period. After the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa|Northern Conquest]] (893–925), the system operated in parallel with the emerging [[Family Rule System]] and later the [[Hoggaanka]] in the frontier territories.

The Church brokered the restoration of [[House Kadim]] circa 963, after the [[House Barkhad]] line ended with [[Bile I]] (r. 949–963). The Church and the [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] families jointly restored the Kadim dynasty to the throne, establishing a coalition between ecclesiastical and feudal interests that would define the politics of the later medieval period.

The [[Hoggaanka]] decree of 1105, issued by [[Marcel II of Burawa|Marcel II]] (r. 1098–1132), established the Church as a constitutional check on royal authority. Under the decree, a [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] could be removed only with the approval of the Burawi Catholic Church, giving the ecclesiastical hierarchy an effective veto over the crown's exercise of power over the feudal aristocracy. Church canon law courts exercised jurisdiction over marriage, inheritance, and moral discipline throughout the kingdom, and Church law prohibited the enslavement of Christians.

The displacement of [[Waaq]] worship followed a regional pattern. On the coast, the older religion declined gradually as Christianity became the religion of the court and the commercial elite. In the highlands, Waaq worship was formally suppressed following the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa|Northern Conquest]], though it persisted in folk practice for centuries. In the northern basin, the indigenous [[Ndogalu]] tradition was largely unaffected during the medieval period; the Christianisation of the north was primarily an Imperial-era undertaking.

=== Imperial period (1180–1516) ===

{{main|Imperial History of Burawa}}

The Church underwent a substantial institutional transformation during the Imperial period. In the early centuries of the era, it continued to exercise the political functions developed during the medieval period: brokering dynastic transitions between cadet houses under the [[Laanta Boqorka]] system, serving as provincial government in the central territories, and administering canon law courts.

The Christianisation of the northern basin constituted the Church's principal ecclesiastical undertaking of the Imperial era. Following the defeat of the [[Lake Confederation]] circa 1180 and the conquest of the [[Kingdom of Shedou]] circa 1200, the Church established missions, parishes, and monasteries across the former Confederation territories. [[Ndogalu]], the indigenous religious tradition of the northern peoples, was suppressed through missionary activity, the construction of churches and monasteries at former Ndogalu centres of worship, and administrative enforcement. Resistance was localised and sporadic. By the mid-13th century, the institutional apparatus of the Church extended throughout the northern territories, though the extent to which Ndogalu practices persisted at the popular level remains a subject of historical debate.

Under the [[Laanta Boqorka]] system, formalised by [[Nuur II of Burawa|Nuur II]] (r. 1232–1268), the Church was empowered to broker the accession of a candidate from one cadet branch when the reigning branch could not furnish an heir. The Church endorsed the transition to [[Kadim-Mazar]] in 1268, restoring [[Kadim-Kaysoor]] in 1355, and the accession of [[Kadim-Galwa]] in 1452. This role as constitutional arbiter extended the Church's political influence throughout the Imperial era and gave the episcopal hierarchy a decisive voice in the succession of kings.

From the mid-15th century, the Church's institutional role shifted from domestic political arbitration toward colonial and missionary activity. The Church endorsed maritime expansion as a means of extending Christianity to previously unreached populations, and contributed to the legitimisation of the colonisation of [[Abyala]] through its declaration that indigenous peoples were suitable subjects for conversion. Church missionaries accompanied the earliest colonial expeditions, and the establishment of parishes in [[Lacashe]] was among the first acts of the colonial administration in 1516. A colonial bishopric was established under the authority of the Cardinal of Burawa, with parishes, schools, and missions operating throughout the colonial territory.

The Church, which had during the medieval period prohibited the enslavement of Christians and imposed minimum standards of treatment, did not extend these protections to non-Christian peoples. The expansion of slavery during the late Imperial period established the institutional and commercial foundations of the [[Kaftian slave trade]] that characterised the colonial era.

=== Late colonial period (1620–1709) ===

{{main|Late Colonial History of Burawa}}

The Church had been a pillar of [[Kadim-Galwa]] legitimacy since the branch's accession in 1608. The Church had supported the Galwa claim against [[Kadim-Kaysoor]] after the [[War of the Mesogean]], and the Galwa kings rewarded the Church with patronage, privileges, and influence in state affairs. The alliance was mutually beneficial: the dynasty relied upon the Church for legitimacy, particularly during succession transitions, while the Church relied upon the crown for protection, patronage, and the enforcement of ecclesiastical authority.

The [[Cathedral of Weli Markos]], constructed between 1662 and 1678 under [[Faarax IV of Burawa|Faarax IV]], represented the apex of [[Mesogean-Kaftian synthesis]] in ecclesiastical architecture. The cathedral, built in the city of [[Weli Markos]] southeast of [[Samara]] on a hill at the bottom of a bay on the coast, featured a vast interior nave, colonnades of local stone, and a façade incorporating both Mesogean classical elements and indigenous Kaftian decorative motifs. The cathedral became the seat of the colonial bishopric and the site of state ceremonies, and the city that grew around it became the de facto religious centre of the kingdom.

The [[Great Testimony]] (1685), a comprehensive historical work compiling the history of Burawa from its founding to the late seventeenth century, was compiled by a team that included Church scholars alongside royal chroniclers and oral historians of the [[gewel]] bardic caste. The Testimony synthesised written records, archival documents, and oral traditions, and served as both a legitimising chronicle of the [[House Kadim|Kadim]] dynasty and an attempt to preserve traditions being lost as the gewel were marginalised.

The Church's 1695 anti-slavery declaration was a watershed in its moral teaching. For nearly two centuries, the Church had accommodated the institution of slavery, baptising enslaved persons during the Middle Passage and operating parishes for enslaved populations in the colonies. The theological justification had been that enslavement facilitated the conversion of non-Christian peoples. The 1695 synod, convened with the approval of the [[Cardinal of Burawa]], reversed this position. Drawing upon the intellectual currents of the [[Mesogean Renaissance]] and the arguments of reformers within the Burawi Church, the bishops declared the slave trade incompatible with Christian ethics. The declaration did not immediately emancipate enslaved persons but announced the Church's intention to phase out the institution and called upon the crown to cease granting licences for the trade. The declaration alienated the merchant class, particularly the houses of [[House Guleed|Guleed]], [[House Warsame|Warsame]], and [[House Ciise|Ciise]], which dominated the slave trade, and strained the Church-Galwa alliance as the crown was caught between its ecclesiastical ally and its financial base.

In 1700, a monastic scholar of the [[Order of Saint Guled]] published the treatise ''[[On the Dignity of All Peoples]]'', arguing that all humans possessed inherent dignity by virtue of their creation in the divine image, and that this dignity was violated by slavery, extreme social hierarchy, and arbitrary power. The treatise circulated widely among the literate classes and became a touchstone for the intellectual currents that would later be recognised as the Burawi precursor to the [[Enlightenment|era of enlightenment]].

The Church played a decisive role in the succession crisis of 1709. Following the death of [[Andrias III of Burawa|Andrias III]] without an heir, the Cardinal of Burawa and the episcopal hierarchy initially recognised the Galwa claim — the child [[Cali IV]], great-nephew of Andrias III — and provided the theological justification for the succession. As the crisis deepened, the Church's position evolved. The Cardinal and his advisors recognised that a civil war would devastate the faithful on both sides and might lead to a permanent rupture in the Burawi Catholic community. The Church ultimately chose reconciliation over division, brokering a settlement that preserved the unity of the Church across the divided kingdom. The Cardinal's seat was formally transferred from [[Samara]] to [[Weli Markos]] as part of the reconciliation, and the Church emerged as the only institution that spanned the political divide, operating from the neutral ground of Weli Markos as a liaison between the eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]] and the western [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]].

=== Four House Era and Constitutional Era (1709–1800) ===

{{main|Four House Era}}{{main|Constitutional Era of Burawa}}

The four-house parliament created under [[Iacob V of Burawa|Iacob V]] following the Imperial Split included a House of the Church, giving the ecclesiastical hierarchy direct legislative representation alongside the crown, the [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] aristocracy, and the landowning merchant class. The Church served as the primary intermediary between the two Burawi kingdoms throughout the era, with the Cardinal of Burawa at Weli Markos maintaining ecclesiastical authority over both states, facilitating communication, mediating disputes, and preserving a sense of shared Burawi Catholic identity across the political divide.

During the [[Restoration Revolution]] (1741–1742), the Church hierarchy opposed the uprising of [[Gali Darg]], not out of loyalty to the dead king, but out of concern for its own position in a Darg-led regime. [[Cardinal Petros III]] served as mediator during the constitutional convention of 1742–1743, his neutrality making him an acceptable arbiter among the competing factions. The [[Constitution of 1743]] confirmed the special position of the Burawi Catholic Church and retained its role in education, marriage, and moral regulation, but the Church, along with the crown, lost its direct legislative representation. The four-house parliament was reduced to two houses — the Hoga Senate and the House of Commons — and the Church became a civil institution subject to parliamentary regulation rather than a co-equal branch of government.

The Constitutional Era saw the gradual secularisation of the Burawi state. Citizenship was extended to non-Catholics, eroding the centuries-old identification of citizenship with membership in the Burawi Catholic Church. Jewish merchants, who had been present in Burawa since the medieval period, and Protestant merchants from [[Illypnia]]n states gained full civil rights. The Church's special position was maintained in principle, but the growing power of commercial interests that had little use for ecclesiastical privilege steadily reduced the Church's practical influence over policy.

The Church continued to mediate between the Burawi kingdoms. In 1822, during the [[Burbur Era]], the Cardinal of Burawa mediated the peace negotiations that ended the [[Burawi War]] (1811–1822) from neutral Weli Markos, producing the settlement that created [[Kijura]] as a buffer state between the two kingdoms.

=== Republic and socialist era (1832–1960s) ===

{{main|Early Republic Era of Burawa}}

The establishment of the [[First Republic of Burawa]] in 1832 reduced the Church's formal role in government. The Church retained its special position in Burawi society — operating schools, registering births and deaths, and providing moral guidance — but the Cardinal of Burawa no longer held a seat in the legislature, and the ecclesiastical hierarchy's direct political authority was curtailed.

The Church lost credibility among segments of the population due to its historical alignment with the landowning elite. The Church had supported the monarchy through its final decades and had been closely associated with the [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] aristocracy. For those disillusioned with the old order, the Church was perceived as part of a discredited establishment. This disillusionment created space for alternative Christian movements, including [[Lathrianism]], a nontrinitarian faith founded in the [[United Kingdom of Jarin and Itegar]] that arrived in Burawa during the 19th century. The Burawi Catholic Church opposed the new movement, but religious protections enacted in the 1800s prevented outright persecution.

The socialist revolution of the early 20th century further diminished the Church's position. [[Manaa Kadar]], the principal revolutionary leader, initially advocated for the abolition of organised religion and the forced closure of churches, arguing that the Burawi Catholic Church's alignment with the landowning elite made it incompatible with the socialist state. [[Kunciil Hanad]] opposed this position, arguing that religious repression would alienate the devout among the non-elite population. The compromise reduced religion's role in public life without banning it outright. The Church's institutional position remained diminished until the reforms of the 1960s, when broader political liberalisation indirectly restored some of the Church's standing by easing restrictions on religious institutions and permitting a greater range of social and cultural activity.

=== Lacashe schism (1789) ===

{{main|Lacashe Church}}

When [[Lacashe]] declared independence from Burawa in 1789 rather than accept the abolition of slavery, the colonial bishopric faced a crisis. The Bishop of Lacashe, [[Filipos of Lacashe|Filipos]], aligned with the planter government and refused to enforce the metropolitan abolition directive. Filipos declared the establishment of an independent [[Lacashe Church]], with himself as its head. The Burawi Catholic Church excommunicated Filipos and declared the Lacashe Church schismatic. The division was primarily political — an assertion of independence from metropolitan authority — rather than theological; the Lacashe Church maintained doctrines and liturgical practices largely identical to those of the Burawi Church. The schism persisted after independence and was not resolved by the [[War of Liberation (1853–1856)|War of Liberation]], though relations between the two churches improved in subsequent decades.

=== Reincorporation of the Lacashe Church (1890s) ===

The reconciliation between the Lacashe Church and the Burawi Catholic Church was a gradual process that unfolded over the decades following the War of Liberation. The abolition of slavery in 1856 removed the original political cause of the schism, and the partition of Lacashe into the freedman state of [[Libertalia]] and the rump Republic of Lacashe weakened the planter elite's institutional grip on the church. The Lacashe Church's hierarchy, which had been closely identified with the slaveholding order, lost both its political rationale and its social base as the old planter class was dispossessed and marginalised.

In the years following the war, the two churches maintained informal contacts through parish-level cooperation and shared pastoral care, particularly in communities where adherents of both communions lived side by side. The Cardinal of Burawa, operating from [[Weli Markos]], kept lines of communication open with the Lacashe bishops without pressing for formal reunification. The Burawi Church's position was that the schism had been political rather than theological, and that the Lacashe Church's continued separation lacked doctrinal justification.

By the 1880s, the generation of clergy that had lived through the schism had largely passed from the scene, and a new cohort of Lacashe bishops was more receptive to reconciliation. The political and social conditions that had sustained the division had been transformed: slavery was abolished, the planter state had collapsed, and the Lacashe Church's independent status had become an anachronism. The practical benefits of reunification — restored connection to the broader Catholic communion, access to the educational and charitable resources of the [[Bartay Society]] and the [[Order of Saint Guled]], and the moral authority of reconciliation with the mother church — outweighed the diminishing reasons for continued separation.

In the early 1890s, formal negotiations between the Cardinal of Burawa and the senior bishops of the Lacashe Church produced an agreement for reincorporation. The Lacashe Church was reintegrated into the Burawi Catholic Church as the [[Diocese of Lacashe]], with its bishops restored to communion with the Cardinal of Burawa and through him with the [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]] hierarchy. The terms of reunification allowed the Lacashe diocese to retain certain local liturgical customs that had developed during the century of separation, and the excommunication of Filipos was posthumously lifted as a gesture of reconciliation. The reincorporation ended a century of ecclesiastical division and restored the institutional unity of the Burawi Catholic Church across its historic territory.

== Organisation and structure ==

The Burawi Catholic Church is headed by the [[Cardinal of Burawa]], whose seat has been at [[Weli Markos]] since 1709. Prior to the Imperial Split, the Cardinal's seat was at [[Samara]], the capital of the unified kingdom. The transfer to Weli Markos was part of the reconciliation settlement that preserved the Church's unity across the divided kingdoms, placing the Cardinal's seat on neutral ground between the eastern and western Burawi states.

The Cardinal of Burawa is appointed in communion with the [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]] hierarchy in Volisania. The Burawi Church recognises the spiritual authority of the [[Pope]] as head of the broader Catholic communion, but governs its internal affairs — liturgy, ecclesiastical appointments, canon law, diocesan organisation — autonomously. This arrangement, established at the Church's founding in 573, has been characterised by varying degrees of Volisanian influence over the centuries, with the Burawi hierarchy consistently asserting its internal autonomy while maintaining communion with the broader Catholic world.

Below the Cardinal, the Church is organised into dioceses corresponding to the major regions and provinces of the historical Burawi state, each headed by a bishop. The colonial bishopric of [[Lacashe]], established in 1516, was the principal overseas diocese until the schism of 1789. Parishes operate at the local level throughout the Church's territory, maintained by diocesan priests. The Church's hierarchy of bishops, monasteries, and parishes was modelled on the Volisanian Catholic structure at its founding and has retained this basic form, though the specific diocesan boundaries and the number of bishoprics have evolved over the centuries.

The Church maintains its own canon law, administered through ecclesiastical courts with jurisdiction over marriage, inheritance, and moral discipline. In the medieval and Imperial periods, canon law courts exercised significant authority alongside secular courts; in the modern era, their jurisdiction is limited to internal Church matters.

== Monastic orders ==

The Burawi Catholic Church has sponsored several monastic orders throughout its history, each with distinct charisms and institutional roles.

=== Order of Saint Guled ===

The [[Order of Saint Guled]] is the oldest Burawi monastic order, traditionally dated to the late 8th century. Founded by [[Saint Guled]], an early Kaftian Christian scholar and missionary who was among the first generation of native-born Burawi clergy, the order is contemplative and scholarly in character. Its monasteries maintained [[scriptorium|scriptoria]] where manuscripts were copied and preserved, producing the principal literary corpus of medieval Burawi. The order's scholars produced theological works, hagiographies, chronicles, and natural philosophy treatises, and the order is credited with preserving much of the pre-Christian Kaftian oral tradition through written compilation.

The Order of Saint Guled is headquartered at the [[Monastery of Saint Guled]] near [[Kaysoor]], founded on the site of an earlier church established during the reign of [[Teodor I of Burawa|Teodor I]]. The order's houses were established throughout the Burawi territories as the kingdom expanded, and the order played a central role in the Christianisation of the northern basin during the Imperial period. The order's scholarly reputation reached its apex in the late 17th century, when a monastic scholar of the order published ''[[On the Dignity of All Peoples]]'' (1700), the most radical intellectual work of the late colonial period.

=== Bartay Society ===

{{main|Bartay Society}}

The [[Bartay Society]] is a fraternal religious order founded in 1611 with the approval of the head of the Burawi Catholic Church. Modelled in part on the institutional methods of the Volisanian [[Jesuits|Jesuit order]], the society was established to support evangelisation, education, and charity within the Burawi sphere. The Bartay Society developed an extensive institutional presence in [[Burawa]], [[Galwa]], [[Bindorama]], and [[Lacashe]], founding schools and charitable establishments that became an important part of the educational infrastructure of the late colonial period. In some areas, the society's schools later adopted more liberal teaching methods than those used by older clerical institutions, exposing Burawi students to broader intellectual currents.

=== Order of Saint Iacob ===

The [[Order of Saint Iacob]] is a missionary order named after [[Iacob I of Burawa|Iacob I]], the first Christian king of Burawa, traditionally dated to the 10th century. The order was founded to support the Christianisation of the highland territories following the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa|Northern Conquest]] (893–925), and its monks accompanied the royal army as it advanced into the [[Kingdom of Ufyen|Ufyen]] highlands. The order's charism combines evangelisation with pastoral ministry in frontier and newly conquered territories. After the Christianisation of the northern basin, the order's mission shifted to the colonial sphere, and its members were among the first missionaries to accompany the colonial expeditions to [[Abyala]] in the early 16th century. The order maintains houses in Burawa, the former colonial territories, and several neighbouring states.

=== Order of the Holy Apostles ===

The [[Order of the Holy Apostles]] is a contemplative and liturgical order associated with the [[Cathedral of the Holy Apostles]] at [[Kaysoor]]. Founded in the late 7th century during the reign of [[Teodor I of Burawa|Teodor I]], the order is dedicated to the maintenance of liturgical worship, the development of Church music, and the preservation of the Burawi liturgical tradition. The order's choirs developed the distinctive Burawi liturgical music that blends [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] choral practice with indigenous Kaftian melodic forms. The order's houses, attached to major cathedrals and churches throughout the Burawi territories, served as centres of liturgical excellence and musical innovation throughout the medieval and Imperial periods.

== Theology and practice ==

The Burawi Catholic Church is a {{W|Trinitarianism|trinitarian}} Catholic communion. It affirms the {{W|Trinity}} — the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit as three persons of one God — and considers itself part of the universal Catholic Church. The Church is in full communion with the [[Petran Catholic Church]] and recognises the spiritual authority of the Pope. The principal theological distinction between the Burawi Catholic Church and the [[Lathrianism|Lathrian]] movement, which arrived in Burawa in the 19th century, is the doctrine of the Trinity: the Burawi Church affirms trinitarian orthodoxy, while Lathrianism is explicitly {{W|Nontrinitarianism|nontrinitarian}}.

Liturgy is conducted in the [[Burawi language]], a practice established at the Church's founding. The Burawi liturgical rite, developed over centuries from Volisanian antecedents, constitutes a distinctive tradition within the broader Catholic communion. The rite incorporates indigenous Kaftian musical and poetic forms alongside the structured liturgical framework inherited from the Volisanian model.

The Church administers the {{W|sacraments}} of baptism, confirmation, eucharist, reconciliation, marriage, holy orders, and anointing of the sick. Canon law, administered through ecclesiastical courts, historically exercised jurisdiction over marriage, inheritance, and moral discipline. In the modern era, the Church's canon law jurisdiction is limited to internal ecclesiastical matters, though the Church continues to offer pastoral guidance on marriage and family life to its adherents.

The Church venerates a calendar of saints that includes both figures from the broader Catholic tradition and Burawi-specific saints. [[Iacob I of Burawa|Saint Iacob]], the first Christian king, is the principal patron saint of the Burawi Catholic Church. [[Saint Guled]], the scholar-missionary and founder of the Order of Saint Guled, is venerated as the patron of learning and scholarship. The Church's sanctoral calendar, developed over centuries, incorporates commemoration of early Kaftian Christian martyrs, medieval bishops and monks, and figures associated with the major events of Burawi Christian history.

== Architecture ==

{{main|Mesogean-Kaftian synthesis}}

Burawi ecclesiastical architecture is characterised by a [[Mesogean-Kaftian synthesis]] that developed over the course of the medieval and Imperial periods. The earliest churches, built in the 6th and 7th centuries, followed the Volisanian {{W|basilica}} plan but were adapted to the Burawi climate with open courtyards, thick walls, and flat or gently pitched roofs. The [[Cathedral of the Holy Apostles]] at [[Kaysoor]], commissioned by [[Teodor I of Burawa|Teodor I]] in the late 7th century, was the finest expression of this early style.

After the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa|Northern Conquest]], the Ufyen ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' dry-stone tradition — massive, unmortared walls of fitted granite — was absorbed into the Burawi architectural vocabulary. The fusion of Mesogean colonnades and arched doorways with highland dry-stone masonry produced a distinctive style visible in the churches of the 10th and 11th centuries.

The [[Cathedral of Weli Markos]], constructed between 1662 and 1678, represented the apex of the Mesogean-Kaftian synthesis in ecclesiastical architecture. The cathedral features a vast interior nave, colonnades of local stone, and a façade incorporating both Mesogean classical elements and indigenous Kaftian decorative motifs. The cathedral has served as the seat of the Cardinal of Burawa since 1709.

== Role in society ==

=== Education ===

Throughout the medieval and Imperial periods, the Burawi Catholic Church was the primary provider of education in the Burawi state. Monastic schools operated by the Church and its orders taught theology, literacy in the [[Mesogean script|Echian script]], mathematics, and [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] languages. The [[Bartay Society]] developed an extensive network of schools in the late colonial period, and the [[Order of Saint Guled]] maintained scriptoria and libraries that preserved the literary heritage of the kingdom. The Church's educational role was reduced under the socialist reforms of the early 20th century, when state schools replaced parochial institutions as the principal providers of education, but the Church continues to operate schools and educational institutions in the modern era.

=== Political mediation ===

The Church served as a constitutional arbiter throughout the medieval and Imperial periods, brokering dynastic transitions under the [[Laanta Boqorka]] system and exercising a veto over the removal of [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] lords under the 1105 decree. After the Imperial Split of 1709, the Church became the primary intermediary between the eastern and western Burawi kingdoms, operating from neutral Weli Markos. The Cardinal of Burawa mediated the peace negotiations that ended the [[Burawi War]] in 1822 and served as a liaison between the two kingdoms until their eventual reconciliation. The Church's political mediation role was reduced under the republic and effectively ended with the socialist revolution, though the Cardinal of Burawa remains a respected public figure.

=== Slavery and abolition ===

The Church's relationship with the institution of slavery evolved significantly over its history. During the medieval period, Church law prohibited the enslavement of Christians and imposed minimum standards of treatment, but did not seek the abolition of the institution. War captives from successive conquests constituted the largest source of enslaved persons, and the kingdom participated in the trans-Mesogean slave trade. The Church did not extend its protections to non-Christian peoples, a distinction that enabled the expansion of the [[Kaftian slave trade]] during the late Imperial and colonial periods.

The 1695 synodal declaration marked a watershed. The bishops declared the slave trade incompatible with Christian ethics and called upon the crown to cease licensing the trade. The declaration was followed by ''[[On the Dignity of All Peoples]]'' (1700), which argued on theological grounds that all humans possessed inherent dignity violated by slavery. The Church's anti-slavery position alienated the merchant class and contributed to the political tensions that culminated in the Imperial Split. The Church's moral authority on the slavery question was a significant factor in the abolitionist movement that ultimately produced the [[Abolition of Slavery Act 1789]].

== Demographics ==

The Burawi Catholic Church remains the largest single Christian communion across the former Burawi sphere, though its adherent population varies significantly by country. In several former Burawi states, the Church retains majority or near-majority adherence; in others, secularisation, the growth of alternative denominations, and socialist-era restrictions on religious practice have reduced its share of the population.

